COURSE_DATA("is2",{"key":"is2","module":"IS","chapters":[{"id":"is-2","title":"Chapter 2 — Human Resources","title_en":"Human Resources","intro":["In the end, information security depends on people. The best firewall cannot stop an employee who sends drawings to a private mailbox. The strictest access control cannot stop a colleague who is tricked into opening the door by social engineering. ISA Chapter 2 \"Human Resources\" follows the employee lifecycle: qualification checks before someone joins (2.1.1), non-disclosure and compliance obligations when the contract is signed (2.1.2), training and awareness during employment (2.1.3), and rules for mobile work outside the security zones (2.1.4).","Most of the four controls in this chapter are led by HR, but information security, IT, legal and line managers must also take part. For automotive suppliers, the most common gaps are: temporary agency workers and outsourced staff are not covered, training is only given to new hires, and there are no written rules for working from home. After this chapter you should be able to identify the security steps that are missing from your own people-management processes."],"blocks":[{"type":"h3","text":"Employee lifecycle and the related ISA controls"},{"type":"table","head":["Lifecycle stage","Main controls","Key actions"],"rows":[["Recruiting and hiring","2.1.1","Identify sensitive jobs, verify identity, background checks"],["Contract and onboarding","2.1.2, 2.1.3","Sign non-disclosure and security compliance obligations, onboarding training"],["During employment","2.1.3, 2.1.4, 4.2.1","Regular and event-driven training, mobile work rules, access rights adjusted to the job"],["Transfer and leaving","2.1.2, 4.1.1, 4.1.3","Reminder of confidentiality on exit, return of identification means, immediate account deactivation"]]},{"type":"callout","style":"info","title":"Who is \"all staff\"?","text":"In principle, the ISA people-related requirements cover permanent employees, fixed-term staff, temporary agency workers, interns, and external people who work on site long-term and can access company information. For external people, you can require their employer by contract to apply equivalent controls, and link this to supplier management in Chapter 6."},{"type":"tip","who":"arale","text":"Note this down! Most Chapter 2 evidence sits with HR: job descriptions, signed confidentiality agreements, training records. Remember to prepare together with HR before the assessment!"}],"controls":[{"id":"2.1.1","title":"Qualification of employees for sensitive work fields","q_en":"To what extent is the qualification of employees for sensitive work fields ensured?","q_zh":"To what extent is the qualification of employees for sensitive work fields ensured?","objective":"Competent, reliable and trustworthy employees are essential for information security within the organization. Therefore, it is important to check the qualifications of potential employees (e.g., applicants) to an appropriate extent.","req":{"must":["Sensitive work fields and jobs are determined.","The requirements for employees with respect to their job profiles are determined and fulfilled.","The identity of potential employees is verified (e.g., checking identity documents)."],"should":["The personal suitability of potential employees is verified by means of simple methods (e.g., job interview).","An extended suitability verification depending on the respective work field and job is conducted (e.g., assessment centre, psychological analysis, checking of references, certificates and diploma, checking of certificates of conduct, checking of professional and private background)."],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.6.1 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 6.2.2 | NIST CSF 2.0 GV.RR-04, PR.AT-02 | BSI IT-Grundschutz ORP.2 | NIST SP800-53r5 PS-2, PS-3, PS-7, PS-9, SA-21","change2027":"","explain":["2.1.1 is about security \"before a person comes through the door\". The three must requirements form a chain: first find out which work fields and jobs are **sensitive** (for example IT system administrators, R&D and design, HR and payroll, finance, staff working with prototypes or test vehicles, people with access to customer networks). Then define the required skills, certificates and trustworthiness for these jobs in the job descriptions. Finally, when hiring, at least **verify the identity**, so you know the applicant is really the person on the ID document.","The should requirements have two levels. General suitability is checked with simple methods (a job interview). For sensitive jobs, extended checks are done based on risk, for example references from former employers, checking that diplomas and certificates are genuine, professional certifications, or even a certificate of good conduct. The original text lists many methods, but it does not require all of them. You choose the right depth \"depending on the work field and job\". What matters is that you have a basis for the decision and apply it consistently.","In practice, background checks must also comply with data-protection and employment law (for example the EU GDPR, or Taiwan's PDPA and Employment Service Act). For example, you may not ask applicants for private data that has nothing to do with the job, and collecting a police certificate or contacting a former employer needs the person's consent and must be limited to what the job requires. We recommend that HR and legal create a \"background check list for sensitive jobs\" together, stating clearly what is checked, which consents are needed and how long the data is kept.","This control also applies to agency workers and external people who work on site long-term. The ISA must requirement says \"potential employees\", and auditors often ask: are outsourced IT engineers and agency operators checked by their agency? Is this agreed in the contract? You can explain this together with supplier management in 6.1.1."],"practice":["Create a \"sensitive jobs list\": job title, department, reason for sensitivity (e.g., system administration rights, access to prototypes, access to customer confidential information) and required check level. Review it once a year as the organization changes.","Add an \"information security requirements\" section to each job description, for example required certificates, documents to sign, and onboarding security training to complete.","At onboarding, HR checks the original ID document and keeps a record of the check (only \"checked, by whom, date\" — no need to copy the whole ID, in line with data minimization).","Before hiring for a sensitive job, run extended checks: verify diplomas and certificates, contact former employers (with written consent), ask for a police certificate where necessary, and keep the results on a \"background check record form\".","Agency and outsourced staff: require the agency or contractor in the contract to perform equivalent checks and provide a declaration. The requesting department confirms this before the person starts on site.","When an employee moves internally into a sensitive job (for example an operator becomes an MES system administrator), check qualifications and check level again, as for a new hire."],"audit":{"questions":["How does your organization define sensitive work fields and jobs? Where is the list?","Please explain the qualification requirements for the system administrator role, and how the most recently hired administrator was verified against them.","How is the identity of new hires verified at onboarding? Who is responsible? Is there a record?","Which extended checks are done for sensitive jobs? How do you get the person's consent?","How are the qualifications of agency workers or on-site outsourced engineers confirmed?","Is a new assessment done when an employee moves internally into a sensitive job?"],"evidence":["Sensitive jobs list and the criteria used","Job descriptions that include security requirements","New-hire onboarding checklist (with an identity check field)","Background check consent forms and check records (sample)","Personnel screening clauses in agency or outsourcing contracts"],"findings":["Job descriptions exist, but no jobs are identified as sensitive. All jobs are treated the same, and there is no basis for the depth of checks.","No record is kept of identity checks for new hires, so there is no proof they were done.","The system administrator job requires a specific certificate, but the sampled person does not have it, and there is no plan to close the gap.","Agency workers start working directly; the contract does not require the agency to perform identity or background checks."]},"tip":"Allan's tip: More background checks are not always better. Check sensitive jobs in depth and other jobs just enough — and always get consent. Otherwise you fix security but create a data-protection problem."},{"id":"2.1.2","title":"Contractual obligation of staff to comply with IS policies","q_en":"To what extent is all staff contractually bound to comply with information security policies?","q_zh":"To what extent is all staff contractually bound to comply with information security policies?","objective":"Organizations are subject to legislation, regulations and internal policies. Already when hiring staff, it must be ensured that employees commit to compliance with the policies and are aware of the consequences of misconduct.","req":{"must":["A non-disclosure obligation is in effect.","An obligation to comply with the information security policies is in effect."],"should":["A non-disclosure obligation beyond the employment contract is in effect.","Information security aspects are considered in the employment contracts of the staff.","A procedure for handling violations of said obligations is described."],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.6.2, A.6.5 | BSI IT-Grundschutz ORP.2 | NIST SP800-53r5 PL-4, PS-4, PS-6, PS-8","change2027":"","explain":["The key words in the control question are **all staff**: not only permanent employees, but also agency workers, interns, student workers and external people working on site long-term. There are only two must requirements, but both must be \"in effect\": the non-disclosure obligation and the obligation to comply with the information security policies. \"In effect\" means signed, dated and traceable to the current staff list — not just a template.","The should requirements go further: the non-disclosure obligation continues after employment ends (for example, trade secrets and customer information remain confidential after the person leaves), security clauses are included in the employment contract or work rules, and there is a procedure for handling violations (for example disciplinary action under the work rules or civil claims), so employees clearly know the consequences.","Note: a common setup is a three-part package of \"employment contract + confidentiality agreement + work rules\". Work rules (in Taiwan, for example, registered with the labor authority) bind all employees and can include compliance with security policies and sanctions for violations. The confidentiality agreement can define a confidentiality period after leaving. Trade secret law (such as the EU Trade Secrets Directive or Taiwan's Trade Secrets Act) also provides a legal basis, but only if the company has taken \"reasonable measures to keep the information secret\" — and signed confidentiality documents are one piece of evidence for such measures.","This control is often linked with 2.1.3 (training), 6.1.2 (NDAs with external parties) and 1.1.1 (publishing the policy). If employees sign that they will \"comply with the security policies\", you must be able to show that the policies are published, accessible to employees and covered in training. Otherwise the contractual obligation will be seen as a formality only."],"practice":["Create an \"employee confidentiality and security compliance agreement\" covering the scope of confidentiality, the period after leaving, compliance with company security policies and procedures, and how violations are handled. It is signed on the first day.","Update the work rules and employment contract templates with security clauses (for example: do not take confidential data out without permission, do not use unapproved software, report security incidents).","Agency workers, interns and on-site contractor staff all sign a personal confidentiality agreement, or their employer signs and provides a list of the people concerned.","Define an \"information security violation procedure\": violation levels, investigation process, sanctions mapped to work-rule clauses, integrated with the HR disciplinary process.","Every year, compare signed records with the current staff list to confirm 100% coverage. At exit, the leaving form reminds the person of the confidentiality obligation and asks them to sign an exit confidentiality statement.","When the policy changes significantly, use electronic sign-off (for example M365 Forms or the HR system) so employees confirm again that they have read it and agree to comply."],"audit":{"questions":["Please show the template for the employee non-disclosure obligation and the signed records of three sampled new hires.","How are agency workers, interns and on-site contractors bound by confidentiality?","Does the confidentiality obligation remain valid after an employee leaves? For how long?","How do employees commit to complying with the security policies? When a policy changes, how are they informed?","What is the procedure if an employee violates a security policy? Have there been any recent cases?"],"evidence":["Employment contract template and work rules (with security clauses)","Confidentiality agreements and signed records (sampled against the staff list)","Exit confidentiality statements","Security violation procedure and disciplinary records (if any)","Confidentiality agreements or contract clauses for external people"],"findings":["All permanent employees signed confidentiality agreements, but agency workers and interns have no confidentiality obligation at all.","The confidentiality clause only applies during employment; there is no obligation after leaving and no reminder at exit.","The contract says to follow company rules, but does not explicitly include the information security policies.","There is no violation procedure. An employee once sent drawings outside without permission and only received a verbal warning, with no record."]},"tip":"Allan's tip: Auditors love to pick random names from the staff list and check the signed records. Do this check yourself once a year — it is much better than being caught with missing documents."},{"id":"2.1.3","title":"Security awareness and training of staff","q_en":"To what extent is staff made aware of and trained with respect to the risks arising from the handling of information?","q_zh":"To what extent is staff made aware of and trained with respect to the risks arising from the handling of information?","objective":"If the requirements and risks of information security are not known to the employees, there is a risk of misconduct resulting in damage to the organization. Therefore, it is important that information security is internalized and practiced as a natural part of their work.","req":{"must":["Employees are trained and made aware."],"should":["A concept for awareness and training of employees is prepared. The following aspects are considered: – Information security policy; – Reporting of information security events; – Reaction to occurrence of malware; – Policies regarding user accounts and login information (e.g., password policy); – Compliance issues of information security; – Requirements and procedures regarding the use of non-disclosure agreements when sharing information requiring protection; – Use of external IT services.","Target groups for training and awareness measures (i.e., people working in specific risk environments such as managers including organizational leaders, administrators, employees having access to customer networks, personnel in areas of manufacturing) are identified and considered in a training concept.","The concept has been approved by the responsible management.","Training and awareness measures are carried out both at regular intervals and in response to events.","Participation in training and awareness measures is documented.","Contact persons for information security are known to employees."],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 7.2, 7.3, A.6.3 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 6.2.4 | NIST CSF 2.0 PR.AT-01, PR.AT-02, GV.RR-01, GV.RR-02, GV.OC-02, GV.RR-04 | BSI IT-Grundschutz ORP.2 | NIST SP800-53r5 AT-2(2), AT-2(3), AT-3, AT-3(5), AT-4, AT-6, IR-2, IR-3, AC-21(1), PM-12, PM-13, SA-16","change2027":"ISA 2027 adds a dedicated training requirement for senior management and executive leadership to the target groups. At the same time, the former 5.2.3 should requirement on \"case-by-case staff awareness\" was deleted; it is now covered by 2.1.3.","explain":["The must requirement is only one sentence: \"Employees are trained and made aware.\" It looks simple, but maturity level 3 expects a systematic training concept, and that means the six should items: a concept, target groups, management approval, regular and event-driven measures, records, and employees who know whom to contact.","The training concept must cover seven topics: the security policy, incident reporting, what to do in case of malware, account and password rules, compliance, signing an NDA before sharing confidential information, and rules for external IT services (for example cloud services and generative AI tools). Auditors often compare these seven topics one by one with the table of contents of your training material, so it is best if your outline maps to them directly.","ISA 2027 puts special focus on **target groups**: managers (including organizational leaders), system administrators, employees with access to customer networks, and staff in manufacturing areas. Senior managers are frequent targets of social engineering (for example CEO fraud or fake payment requests). Production staff may have no computer and rarely read e-mail, so the training format must be adapted, for example short talks in shift meetings, posters and short videos.","\"In response to events\" means targeted awareness after a security incident, a new threat (for example a large phishing wave) or a policy change. This is also where maturity levels differ: annual training only shows that something is \"performed\"; adjusting quickly to events shows that it is really \"managed\"."],"practice":["Create an \"annual security training plan\": target groups, topics, format, frequency, owner and completion target, approved by the CISO or the managing director.","New hires complete basic security training (online course plus test) within one week of joining. Until they complete it, they do not get access to confidential folders.","All staff receive security awareness training at least once a year, plus quarterly phishing simulations (for example with M365 Defender Attack Simulation or a third-party platform). People who click receive follow-up training.","Target-group training: senior managers focus on social engineering and crisis decisions; system administrators focus on privileged accounts and change management; production staff get five-minute talks in shift meetings and rules for USB devices and OT equipment.","Event-driven awareness: after an incident or a new type of fraud, publish a security alert within 48 hours (Teams, an official chat channel such as WhatsApp or LINE, notice boards).","Keep training records and test results in an LMS or on attendance lists. Calculate the completion rate every year and include it in the management review.","Show the security contact and how to report on the intranet home page, notice boards and desktop wallpaper (phone extension, e-mail, form link)."],"audit":{"questions":["Please show this year's security training plan. Who approved it?","Does the training cover incident reporting, malware response, password policy, NDAs and the use of external IT services?","Is there different training for managers, system administrators and production staff?","After the most recent security incident or threat, was there targeted awareness communication?","Please show the attendance records. What happens to people who did not attend?","(Interviewing an employee on site) If you suspected your computer had malware, what would you do? Who is your security contact?"],"evidence":["Approved annual security training plan","Training material and outline (mapped to the seven topics)","Attendance lists or LMS completion reports, test results","Phishing simulation reports and follow-up training records","Security alerts published after events","Screenshots or photos of posters showing the security contact"],"findings":["Only new hires are trained; current employees have not received any security training for years.","The training plan is not approved by management and no target groups are identified; managers and production staff all take the same course.","Training records are incomplete, so the completion rate cannot be proven, and people who did not attend are not followed up.","Employees interviewed on site do not know whom to report a security incident to.","The training material does not cover rules for external IT services (such as free cloud storage or AI tools)."]},"tip":"Allan's tip: Auditors randomly ask employees on site \"Who do you call if you get malware?\" Not knowing the answer hurts more than a missing attendance list. Put the contact details where everyone can see them."},{"id":"2.1.4","title":"Regulation of mobile work","q_en":"To what extent is mobile work regulated?","q_zh":"To what extent is mobile work regulated?","objective":"Working outside the specifically defined security zones (mobile work) creates risks requiring corresponding protective measures.","req":{"must":["The requirements for mobile work are determined and fulfilled. The following aspects are considered: – Behavior in private surroundings (e.g., working in a dedicated office at home or in a coworking space); – Behavior in public surroundings (e.g., during travels, shared office in a coworking space); – Secure handling of and access to information (in both electronic and paper form) while considering the protection needs and the contractual requirements applying to private and public surroundings; – Protection from theft in public surroundings; – Measures to ensure a secure access to organization’s IT-services."],"should":["The following aspects are considered: – Measures for travelling (e.g., viewing by authorities); – Measures for travelling to security-critical countries.","Employee awareness."],"high":["Protective measures against overhearing and viewing are implemented. (C)"],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.6.7, A.8.1 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 8.4.2 | BSI IT-Grundschutz INF.8, INF.9, CON.7, NET.3.3 | NIST SP800-53r5 PE-17","change2027":"ISA 2027 rewrote the wording of the must requirement. It now clearly separates private surroundings (including home office and coworking space) from public surroundings, and adds measures for secure access to the organization's IT services. ISA 2027.1 also adds \"coworking space\" and \"office-less\" to the definitions.","explain":["Mobile work means handling company information outside the security zones defined by the company: working from home, in a coworking space, on a train during a business trip, at an airport or in a customer's meeting room. The 2.1.4 must requirement lists the five aspects to consider very clearly: behavior in private surroundings, behavior in public surroundings, secure handling of information (including paper), theft protection, and secure access to company IT services.","Note the ISA 2027.1 definition of a coworking space: a shared office environment provided by a third party that is not part of the organization's ISMS. In other words, a coworking space is not a company security zone. The original text places it in both private surroundings (a dedicated office) and public surroundings (shared open seating), which means you must decide the protection level based on the actual situation.","\"Considering the protection needs and the contractual requirements\" is important. Many OEM project contracts require that confidential data is processed only on company premises, or forbid opening it in public places. Your mobile work rules must reflect such contractual limits, for example: drawings classified as \"strictly confidential\" may not be opened or printed at home or in public.","The should requirements mention possible viewing by authorities during travel (for example being asked to unlock a laptop at the border) and measures for travel to security-critical countries. A common solution is a clean travel laptop (loaner laptop). High protection needs (C) require protection against overhearing and viewing, for example privacy screen filters, no confidential calls in public places, and headsets for video meetings."],"practice":["Create a \"mobile work policy\": scope (home, coworking, travel, customer site), approval process, which data classes may be handled, and what is forbidden.","Technical measures: full-disk encryption on laptops (BitLocker or FileVault), access to internal systems via VPN or ZTNA with MFA, compliance management with Intune or another MDM, and non-compliant devices blocked (conditional access).","Paper documents: confidential paper documents are generally not taken home. If necessary, they are registered and kept in a lockable container. Documents to be destroyed are brought back to the office for shredding, never thrown into household waste.","Theft protection: never leave a laptop unattended in public places or visible in a car. Loss must be reported within the defined time (linked to 1.6.1).","High protection needs: provide privacy screen filters and headsets, do not hold confidential meetings in open spaces, and use background blur or a virtual background.","Travel rules: for high-risk countries, use dedicated travel laptops and phones, carry only the data you need, and reinstall or check the devices after returning. Explain how to act and report if customs inspects a device.","Raise awareness with annual training and a mobile work self-check list (for example: has the default password of the home router been changed, is there a separate workspace)."],"audit":{"questions":["Does the company allow working from home or in coworking spaces? Where are the rules?","How do employees access company systems from home or while travelling? Is MFA used?","Do customer contracts restrict where data may be processed? How do the mobile work rules reflect this?","May confidential paper documents be taken out of the company? How is this controlled?","If an employee loses a laptop on a train or at an airport, what must they do?","Are there special measures for business trips to high-risk countries?","(High protection needs) How do you prevent overhearing and viewing in public places?"],"evidence":["Mobile work policy or home office rules","VPN or ZTNA and MFA configuration screenshots, conditional access policies","Device encryption status report (Intune or MDM)","Loan records for dedicated travel devices","Records of privacy filter distribution, mobile work training records"],"findings":["The company actually allows working from home, but there are no written rules, only verbal approval by managers.","The rules only cover electronic data, not taking paper documents out or destroying them.","VPN login uses only username and password, with no additional authentication.","The customer contract forbids processing drawings outside the company, but the mobile work rules do not reflect this, and R&D staff open them at home as usual."]},"tip":"Allan's tip: Since the pandemic, many companies work from home \"by habit\" but never wrote it down as a rule. A TISAX auditor who sees VPN connection logs will ask about it, so first document the current situation, then improve it."}],"summary":["2.1.1: First identify sensitive jobs, then set qualification requirements in the job descriptions. Verify the identity of every potential employee; for sensitive jobs, add risk-based extended checks, and respect the consent rules of data-protection law.","2.1.2: The non-disclosure obligation and the obligation to comply with security policies must be \"in effect\" and cover all staff, including agency workers and external people. Ideally they continue after the person leaves, and there is a procedure for handling violations.","2.1.3: Training must be planned, approved by management, cover the seven main topics, target specific groups (including senior management and production staff), run regularly and in response to events, be recorded, and employees must know their security contact.","2.1.4: Mobile work must consider private and public surroundings, paper and electronic information, theft protection and secure remote access. For high protection needs, protect against overhearing and viewing.","Key ISA 2027 changes: 2.1.3 adds dedicated training for senior management, and the 2.1.4 must requirement was rewritten and introduces the coworking space concept."]},{"id":"is-3","title":"Chapter 3 — Physical Security","title_en":"Physical Security","intro":["Chapter 3 is about the security you can \"see and touch\": security zones, supporting assets, and mobile devices that leave the company. Physical security is especially important in the automotive supply chain, because drawings, samples and test data often exist at the same time on paper, on hard drives, on laptops and as physical parts. If any one of these carriers gets out of control, the information leaks.","This chapter covers 3.1.1 security zone management, 3.1.3 handling of supporting assets, and 3.1.4 mobile IT devices and mobile data storage devices. Please note: the former 3.1.2 has been superseded. Its content was split into 1.6.3 (crisis management), 5.2.8 (IT service continuity planning) and 5.2.9 (backup and recovery), so this chapter no longer covers 3.1.2 separately. Also, if your assessment scope includes prototype protection, physical security must be combined with the stricter requirements of Chapter 8 (the 8.2 series)."],"blocks":[{"type":"h3","text":"Example security zone classification (based on the examples in the ISA)"},{"type":"table","head":["Typical color","Protection needs","Example areas"],"rows":[["Gray","Public","Public roads, unfenced parking lots, stairwells in multi-tenant buildings"],["Green","Normal","Fully fenced plant grounds, reception, meeting rooms, corridors"],["Purple","Special","Server rooms, network wiring closets, chemical stores"],["Yellow / orange","High (confidential)","HR, design department, IT office, management offices"],["Red","Very high (strictly confidential)","Customer project offices"]]},{"type":"p","text":"The ISA describes the protection of each zone with four kinds of measures: technical, physical, personnel and organizational. Example for a design room with high protection needs: motion-sensor alarm after working hours; cleaning only during working hours and only with an employee present; no keys handed out to the cleaning company; no photography; access rights reviewed every year; confidential documents locked away after work; visitors escorted at all times."},{"type":"callout","style":"warn","title":"3.1.2 has been superseded","text":"In ISA 2027, 3.1.2 is marked \"Superseded by 1.6.3, 5.2.8 and 5.2.9\". If older documents still refer to 3.1.2, update your mapping table to avoid confusion during the assessment."},{"type":"tip","who":"allan","text":"Security zones are like an onion: one layer inside the next. Auditors love to look for \"holes in the onion\", such as the back door of the goods receiving area or the kitchen next to the server room. Check these places with extra care."}],"controls":[{"id":"3.1.1","title":"Management of security zones","q_en":"To what extent are security zones managed to protect information assets?","q_zh":"To what extent are security zones managed to protect information assets?","objective":"Security zones provide physical protection of information assets. The more sensitive the information assets to be processed are the more protective measures are required.","req":{"must":["A security zone concept including the associated protective measures based on the requirements for the handling of information assets is in place. The following aspects are considered: – Physical conditions (e.g., premises / buildings / spaces) in the definition of security zones; – Delivery and shipping areas.","The defined protective measures are implemented.","The code of conduct for security zones is known to all persons involved."],"should":["Procedures for allocation and revocation of access rights are established.","Visitor management policies (including registration and escorting of visitors) are defined.","Policies for carrying along and using mobile IT devices and mobile data storage devices (e.g., registration before they are carried along, identification obligations) are defined and implemented.","Network/infrastructure components (own or customer networks) are protected against unauthorized access.","External properties used for storing and processing information assets are considered in the security zone concept (e.g. storage rooms, garages, workshops, test tracks, data processing centres)."],"high":["Protective measures against simple overhearing and viewing are implemented. (C)"],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.7.1–A.7.6 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 6.4.1 | NIST CSF 2.0 GV.OC-04, PR.AA-06, DE.CM-02, DE.CM-03 | BSI IT-Grundschutz ORP.1, INF.1, INF.2, INF.5–INF.10, OPS.2.1 | NIST SP800-53r5 PE-2, PE-3, PE-6, PE-8, etc.","change2027":"","explain":["Security zones are areas arranged in layers, one inside the other, separated by barriers and access control, to protect information assets physically. Think of an onion: the outer layer is the public area, then the plant grounds, then the office area, and at the core the server room or the customer project room. The further in you go, the fewer people may enter and the stricter the controls.","The must requirements have three key points. First, a written security zone concept based on physical conditions (site, buildings, floors, rooms) that explicitly includes the **delivery and shipping areas**, because this is where external people (logistics staff, drivers) come and go most often, and it is the gap most often forgotten. Second, the measures in the concept must really be implemented. Third, all persons involved (including cleaning, security guards and contractors) must know the code of conduct for each zone.","The ISA provides an example of common zone colors: gray public areas (unfenced parking lots, public roads), green normal protection areas (fully fenced plant grounds, reception, meeting rooms, corridors), purple special protection areas (server rooms, network wiring closets, chemical stores), yellow or orange high protection areas (HR, design department, IT office, management offices), and red very high protection areas (customer project offices). The colors are only a convention. What matters is that each zone has matching technical, physical, personnel and organizational measures.","The should requirements cover granting and revoking access rights, visitor registration and escort, rules for bringing in mobile devices, protection of network equipment (for example locked wiring cabinets and disabled unused network ports), and external properties (warehouses, test tracks, external data centers) in the concept. High protection needs (C) require protection against simple overhearing and viewing, for example sound-insulated meeting rooms, frosted film on glass walls and cleaning whiteboards after meetings. Prototype protection has even stricter requirements in the 8.2 series."],"practice":["Draw a \"security zone floor plan\" with colors for each protection level, and add a \"security zone table\" listing for each zone the asset types, who may enter, access control method, monitoring method and code of conduct.","Delivery and shipping areas: set up a receiving area separated from the plant. Drivers may not enter production or office areas. Shipping labels and delivery notes are not left where the public can see them.","Access control: use an access card system with rights set per zone. The zone owner approves requests, rights are revoked on the day of leaving or transfer, and the access list is reviewed every year (at least once a year for high protection zones).","Visitor management: register visitors (name, company, host, time), visitors wear a badge, are escorted at all times in high protection zones, sign a confidentiality statement where necessary, and return the badge when they leave.","Mobile devices and photography: no photography in high protection zones, with signs posted; cover visitors' phone cameras with seal stickers or have phones deposited.","Network and infrastructure: lock server rooms and wiring cabinets, disable unused network ports or enable 802.1X, and keep customer leased-line equipment in a controlled area.","High protection needs: sound insulation for meeting and design rooms, frosted windows or blinds, and screens positioned so they do not face windows or corridors."],"audit":{"questions":["Which security zones do you have? How were they designed? Why were they divided this way?","Where are the areas with very high protection needs?","What happens in case of an intrusion?","How many people have access rights to a given zone? Who approved them?","When was the last review of access rights? What was the most recent revocation?","How are the request, approval and issue of access rights recorded?","How does the visitor registration process work?"],"evidence":["Security zone concept and floor plan (site layout)","Key management list","Extract of the electronic access control system's rights list and access logs for specific zones","Visitor management rules and visitor log","Rules for mobile IT devices","Access rights review records","Photos of zones showing the protective measures","Contract with the security company"],"findings":["A security zone floor plan exists, but it does not cover the delivery and shipping areas, and drivers can walk straight into the warehouse and offices.","The security zone concept says there is access control, but on site the server room door is unlocked or kept open with a door wedge.","Access rights have never been reviewed, and access cards of former employees still work.","An external warehouse or test site stores customer parts but is not included in the security zone concept.","Cleaning staff have keys to high protection zones and can enter when nobody is there; this was not assessed in the concept."]},"tip":"Allan's tip: Auditors often walk from the main gate all the way to the server room, comparing your floor plan as they go. Make sure the plan matches reality — do not show an old plan from before the renovation."},{"id":"3.1.3","title":"Handling of supporting assets","q_en":"To what extent is the handling of supporting assets managed?","q_zh":"To what extent is the handling of supporting assets managed?","objective":"During their lifecycle (e.g., usage, disposal), supporting assets are subject to risks such as loss, theft or unauthorized viewing.","req":{"must":["The requirements for the handling of supporting assets (e.g., transport, storage, repair, loss, return, disposal) are determined and fulfilled."],"should":[],"high":["Disposal of supporting assets is conducted in accordance with one of the relevant standards (e.g. ISO 21964, at least security level 4). (C)"],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.5.10, A.5.11 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 9.2.2 | BSI IT-Grundschutz SYS.4.5, CON.6 | ISO/IEC 21964-1:2018-08 | NIST SP800-53r5 MP-6(2), PE-4, PE-5","change2027":"","explain":["Supporting assets are the electronic or physical carriers used to store, process or transmit information assets, for example mobile storage devices, IT systems, IT services and service providers, and paper documents. Information assets are the \"content\", supporting assets are the \"container\". At every step — transport, storage, repair, loss, return and disposal — the container can cause the content to leak.","There is only one must requirement, but the six lifecycle steps in brackets are the auditor's checklist: transport (for example sending a hard drive to a customer), storage (confidential documents locked away), repair (is the hard drive removed or encrypted before a laptop is sent to the manufacturer?), loss (reporting process), return (leased equipment, returning devices when leaving) and disposal (destroying hard drives and paper).","High protection needs (C) require disposal according to a standard, for example ISO 21964 (formerly the German DIN 66399), at least security level 4. ISO 21964 classifies by material — for example paper, optical discs, magnetic media, hard drives and electronic storage media — each with a different particle size. If disposal is outsourced, make sure the provider can prove the required level and issues destruction certificates.","Note: in the ISA, the \"possible questions\" listed under 4.1.1 are actually about supporting assets (for example: what happens to documents when you leave your desk, how are hard drives disposed of, where is the shredder). This course places them under 3.1.3. This control is also related to 1.3.1 asset inventory, 3.1.4 mobile devices, and 5.3.3 return and deletion of data from external IT services."],"practice":["Create a \"supporting asset policy\" that defines requirements for transport, storage, repair, loss, return and disposal by asset type (laptops, server hard drives, USB drives, paper, mobile phones, leased multifunction printers).","Define which supporting assets may store which classes of information, for example: confidential data may not be stored on personal USB drives.","Clear desk policy: when leaving the desk or at the end of the day, lock confidential paper documents in a cabinet, lock the screen (Windows+L), and use badge release for printing.","Repair control: before repair, back up and remove or encrypt the storage media; outsourced repairers sign confidentiality clauses; on-site repairs are escorted.","Disposal process: hard drives are degaussed, physically destroyed or wiped with a standard-compliant tool. For high protection needs, follow ISO 21964 at least level 4; outsourced destruction requires a destruction certificate; paper goes into a shredder of the right level or a locked confidential waste bin.","Leased equipment (multifunction printers, laptops): wipe internal hard drives before return, and define the responsibility for data deletion in the lease contract.","For shipping equipment, use encrypted media and trackable logistics, and record sender, recipient and receipt confirmation."],"audit":{"questions":["Where have you defined which supporting assets exist and which information assets may be stored on them?","How are supporting assets with high protection needs protected? Which encryption methods are used?","Where are the lifecycle requirements for the different supporting assets documented?","What do employees do with documents when they leave their desk?","How are the hard drives of purchased or leased equipment disposed of?","How are paper documents destroyed? Where are the shredders or confidential waste bins?"],"evidence":["Supporting asset policy","Asset inventory (including supporting assets and the information classes stored)","Disposal records and destruction certificates (including the standard and security level used)","Repair records and confidentiality clauses","Shredder specifications, photos of confidential waste bin locations","Records of data deletion before returning leased equipment"],"findings":["Old computers are handed directly to a recycler without wiping the hard drives and without a destruction certificate.","The shredder cuts only long strips and cannot reach the security level required for high protection needs.","A leased multifunction printer was returned without wiping its internal hard drive, although many confidential drawings had been scanned on it.","A laptop was sent to the manufacturer for repair with an unencrypted hard drive and no confidentiality agreement."]},"tip":"Allan's tip: What I see most often during audits is a \"recycling paper box\" in the corner of the office, full of price quotations. Replace it with a locked confidential waste bin."},{"id":"3.1.4","title":"Management of mobile IT devices and mobile data storage devices","q_en":"To what extent is the handling of mobile IT devices and mobile data storage devices managed?","q_zh":"To what extent is the handling of mobile IT devices and mobile data storage devices managed?","objective":"Mobile IT devices (e.g., notebooks, tablets, smartphones) and mobile data storage devices (e.g., SD cards, hard drives) are generally used not only on the premises of an organization, but also in mobile applications. This presents an increased risk of loss, theft, etc.","req":{"must":["The requirements for mobile IT devices and mobile data storage devices are determined and fulfilled. The following aspects are considered: – Encryption; – Access protection (e.g., PIN, password); – Marking (also considering requirements for use in the presence of customers)."],"should":["Registration of the IT devices."],"high":["General encryption of mobile data storage devices or the information assets stored thereon. Where this is technically not feasible, information is protected by similarly effective measures. (C, I)"],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.6.7, A.7.10, A.8.1 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 9.2.2 | BSI IT-Grundschutz SYS.4.5, SYS.3.1, SYS.3.2.1, SYS.3.3 | NIST SP800-53r5 SC-41, SC-42","change2027":"","explain":["3.1.4 focuses on devices that \"travel with you\": laptops, tablets and phones, as well as USB flash drives, SD cards and external hard drives. The must requirement asks for three things: encryption, access protection and marking. These must be \"determined and fulfilled\", so you need written rules and you must be able to show the actual settings on the devices.","Marking has two meanings. First, an asset label, which helps with inventory and with identifying the owner if the device is lost. Second, the original text explicitly mentions requirements for \"use in the presence of customers\". For example, a laptop with a sticker showing the project name of OEM A, taken to a meeting at OEM B, leaks confidential information. Some OEMs also require that supplier devices used at their sites carry specific markings or are registered. Marking must allow identification without revealing confidential information.","The should requirement asks for device registration, meaning an inventory that shows the custodian, serial number and encryption status of each device. High protection needs (C, I) require **general encryption** of mobile storage devices, for example allowing only hardware-encrypted USB drives or enforcing BitLocker To Go. If some devices (for example the SD card of a measuring instrument) cannot be encrypted for technical reasons, protect them with similarly effective measures, such as using them only inside a security zone, wiping them right after use and keeping them locked away.","This control is related to 2.1.4 mobile work, 3.1.3 supporting assets, 4.1.2 authentication and 5.1.1 cryptography. Common blind spots in factories are USB drives used by production or quality staff to copy programs and measurement data, and development boards and test phones used by R&D. These are also within the scope of mobile devices."],"practice":["Create a \"mobile devices and portable storage media policy\": allowed device types, encryption method, PIN and password rules, marking, registration and loss reporting process.","Enforce BitLocker (Windows) or FileVault (macOS) on laptops, report encryption status via Intune or another MDM, and store recovery keys centrally in Entra ID or AD.","Enroll phones and tablets in an MDM (for example Intune or Jamf), enforce screen lock and PIN, and enable remote wipe. For BYOD, use app protection policies to separate company data.","USB control: block unauthorized USB devices with EDR or device control policies and allow only registered encrypted USB drives. For high protection needs, enforce encryption everywhere.","Use neutral numbers on asset labels (for example NB-0123), never customer or project names. Before visiting a customer, check that the wallpaper and login screen do not reveal information about other customers.","Keep a mobile device inventory with serial number, model, custodian, encryption status, MDM enrollment status, and issue and return dates. Check it every six months.","For devices that cannot be encrypted (such as SD cards in measuring equipment), use alternative measures — locked storage, use only in defined areas, wiping after use — and document them in the risk assessment."],"audit":{"questions":["Which mobile IT devices and portable storage devices does the company have? Where is the inventory?","Please show the encryption status of one laptop on site, and the overall encryption rate in the MDM report.","How is access protection on phones configured? Can they be wiped remotely if lost?","Can USB flash drives be used freely? How are they controlled?","How are devices marked? Are there special rules for taking them to customer sites?","(High protection needs) What alternative measures are used for storage devices that cannot be encrypted?"],"evidence":["Mobile device policy","Mobile device inventory","MDM or Intune compliance and encryption status reports","Screenshots of USB control policy settings and records of approved exceptions","Photos of asset labels","Loss reports and remote wipe records"],"findings":["Most laptops are encrypted, but several laptops of managers or R&D staff are not encrypted and not registered.","USB use is not controlled at all; production staff copy programs and measurement data with personal flash drives.","Phones can receive company e-mail but are not enrolled in MDM and have no screen lock requirement.","A laptop carries a sticker with a customer project name and is taken to meetings with other customers."]},"tip":"Allan's tip: An encryption rate of 98% sounds good, but the auditor will ask straight away who the remaining 2% are. Prepare the exception list in advance and explain your plan to fix it."}],"summary":["3.1.1: You need a written security zone concept (including physical conditions and delivery and shipping areas), the measures must be implemented, and all persons involved must know the code of conduct. Should covers granting and revoking access, visitors, mobile devices, network equipment and external properties.","3.1.1 high protection needs: protect against simple overhearing and viewing.","3.1.2 has been superseded by 1.6.3, 5.2.8 and 5.2.9.","3.1.3: Rules are needed for transport, storage, repair, loss, return and disposal of supporting assets. For high protection needs, disposal follows a standard such as ISO 21964, at least security level 4.","3.1.4: Mobile devices and storage devices must consider encryption, access protection and marking (including use in front of customers). Should: register them. High protection needs: full encryption of mobile storage devices or equivalent measures."]},{"id":"is-4","title":"Chapter 4 — Identity and Access Management","title_en":"Identity and Access Management","intro":["Chapter 4 is the chapter IT departments know best — and also the one where findings are most common. Identity and access management (IAM) answers three questions: who are you (identification and authentication), how is your account managed (account lifecycle), and what are you allowed to do (access rights). The ISA divides it into 4.1 Identity Management (4.1.1 identification means, 4.1.2 user authentication, 4.1.3 user accounts and login information) and 4.2 Access Management (4.2.1 access rights).","For small and mid-sized suppliers with only a few IT staff, the challenge in this chapter is \"making it a process\". Accounts, access rights, access cards and tokens are often handled by different people in different ways, without consistent request, approval and review records. We recommend starting with the leaver and transfer processes and connecting HR, facilities and IT into one chain. The main ISA 2027 changes are in 4.1.1 (the time limit for high protection needs was removed) and 4.1.2 (the password requirement became a separate should item, and the wording changed to recognized best practices)."],"blocks":[{"type":"h3","text":"Three layers of IAM"},{"type":"table","head":["Layer","Question","ISA control","Common tools"],"rows":[["Identification means","What do you use to prove who you are?","4.1.1","Access cards, keys, FIDO keys, smart cards"],["Authentication","How do we confirm it is really you?","4.1.2","Passwords, MFA, PAM, conditional access"],["Account","How is an account created and ended?","4.1.3","AD, Entra ID, HR system integration"],["Authorization","What are you allowed to do?","4.2.1","AD groups, RBAC, Purview labels"]]},{"type":"callout","style":"ok","title":"Joiner – Mover – Leaver","text":"In practice, the JML model ties this chapter together. Joiner: create accounts by role and issue identification means. Mover: adjust access rights and remove old ones. Leaver: disable accounts on the last day, collect identification means, and ask customers to disable accounts in customer systems."},{"type":"tip","who":"arale","text":"I get it now! The account is the \"ticket\", and the access rights decide \"which halls the ticket lets you into\". You have to manage both!"}],"controls":[{"id":"4.1.1","title":"Management of identification means","q_en":"To what extent is the use of identification means managed?","q_zh":"To what extent is the use of identification means managed?","objective":"To check the authorization for both physical access and electronic access, means of identification such as keys, visual IDs, or other physical access devices, as well as cryptographic tokens are often used. These security features are only reliable if the use of such identification means is handled adequately.","req":{"must":["The requirements for the handling of identification means over the entire lifecycle are determined and fulfilled. The following aspects are considered: – Creation, handover, return and destruction; – Validity periods; – Traceability; – Handling of loss."],"should":["Identification means can be produced under controlled conditions only."],"high":["A strategy of blocking or invalidation of identification means in case of loss is prepared and implemented as far as possible. (C, I, A)"],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.5.18 | NIST CSF 2.0 PR.AA-01 | BSI IT-Grundschutz SYS.4.5, SYS.3.1, SYS.3.2.1, SYS.3.3 | NIST SP800-53r5 SC-41, SC-42","change2027":"ISA 2027 removed the high protection needs requirement that identification means must be time-limited. Validity periods are still one of the aspects to consider in the must requirement.","explain":["Identification means include physical and electronic items: physical keys, access cards, employee badges and visitor badges, as well as smart cards, hardware tokens, FIDO2 security keys and OTP generators. They show that \"the holder is allowed in\". If their management gets out of control, access control and MFA become useless.","The must requirement asks you to manage the entire lifecycle and lists four aspects: creation, handover, return and destruction; validity periods (for example visitor badges valid for one day, contractor access cards valid for the contract period); traceability (you know which card or key is held by whom); and handling of loss (reporting, blocking, reissue).","The should requirement says identification means may only be produced under controlled conditions, for example access cards issued in the system by facilities, the card printer locked away and blank cards counted; keys of a patented system that cannot be copied without authorization. High protection needs (C, I, A) require a prepared strategy to block or invalidate identification means when lost, for example blocking an access card immediately in the system or revoking a security key in Entra ID. For traditional keys that cannot be disabled remotely, the strategy may be to replace the lock cylinder.","ISA 2027 removed the \"time limit\" requirement for high protection needs, but validity periods are still an aspect of the must requirement, so you still need to define validity periods for each type of identification means. This control is closely related to 3.1.1 access rights, 4.1.3 account management and the leaver process: when people leave, badges, keys and tokens must be returned and disabled in the system at the same time."],"practice":["Create an \"identification means register\": type (key, access card, badge, token, FIDO key), number, holder, issue date, validity period, return date.","Facilities or the security team issue identification means centrally; the card printer and blank cards are locked away. Use patented keys that cannot be copied freely; copies require an approved request.","Visitor badges are collected every day; contractor access cards get an expiry date and stop working automatically; employee card rights are adjusted when the person's job changes.","Handling of loss: employees report a loss immediately; the access card is blocked in the system right away; tokens or FIDO keys are revoked in the identity system and re-enrolled; if a key to an important area is lost, assess replacing the lock cylinder.","Add a \"return of identification means\" field to the leaver form, completed on the same day as account deactivation and signed off by facilities.","Damaged or collected cards and tokens are physically destroyed, and the destruction is recorded.","Check the register once a year against the staff list and the access control system."],"audit":{"questions":["Which identification means does the company use? Who issues and manages them?","Where are the records of issuing, returning and destroying access cards and keys?","How are the validity periods of visitor badges and contractor access cards set?","Please describe how the most recent loss of an identification means was handled.","How are blank access cards and card printers stored? Can keys be copied freely?","(High protection needs) How are lost identification means blocked or invalidated? How fast?"],"evidence":["Identification means policy and register","Access card issue and deactivation records (reports from the access control system)","Key issue and return log","Loss reports and blocking records","Return records for identification means on leaver forms","Token or FIDO key enrollment and revocation records"],"findings":["Departments have keys cut on their own; facilities does not know how many keys exist or who holds them.","A former employee did not return the badge, and the access card still opens the plant gate.","Visitor badges are not numbered and not collected, so anyone who has one can get in.","An employee reported a lost access card only a week later; the card remained active during that time and nobody followed up."]},"tip":"Allan's tip: During a consulting job I once asked facilities \"How many keys are there to the server room?\" The answer was \"Probably three.\" In the end we found seven. Start building your register today."},{"id":"4.1.2","title":"Secure user access to IT services and IT systems","q_en":"To what extent is the user access to IT services and IT systems secured?","q_zh":"To what extent is the user access to IT services and IT systems secured?","objective":"Only securely identified (authenticated) users are to gain access to IT systems. For this purpose, the identity of a user is securely determined by suitable procedures.","req":{"must":["The procedures for user authentication are selected based on a risk assessment. Possible attack scenarios are considered (e.g., direct accessibility via the internet).","Authentication procedures are consistent with recognized security best practices."],"should":["The user authentication procedures are defined and implemented based on the business-related and security-relevant requirements.","Users are authenticated at least by means of strong passwords according to proven and widely accepted practices.","Superior procedures are used for the authentication of privileged user accounts (e.g., privileged access management, two-factor authentication)."],"high":["Depending on the risk assessment, authentication procedure and access control have been enhanced by supplementary measures (e.g., continuous access monitoring with respect to irregularities or use of strong authentication, automatic logout, disabling in case of inactivity, or brute force prevention). (C, I, A)"],"vhigh":["Before gaining access to data of very high protection needs, users are authenticated by means of strong authentication (e.g., two-factor authentication) (C, I)"],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.5.15, A.8.5 | NIST CSF 2.0 PR.AA-02, PR.AA-03 | BSI IT-Grundschutz ORP.4, OPS.1.1.2 | NIST SP800-53r5 AC-2(12), AC-3(14), AC-7, AC-11, AC-12, AC-17, IA-5, IA-11, etc.","change2027":"ISA 2027 changed the should item on \"passwords according to the state of the art\" from a sub-point (–) to a separate requirement (+). ISA 2027.1 also replaced the term \"state-of-the-art\" with \"proven and widely accepted practices\" and \"recognized security best practices\".","explain":["4.1.2 answers the question: when a user logs in, how do you know it is really that person? The must requirement does not say directly whether to use passwords or MFA. Instead it asks for two things: the authentication method is chosen based on a **risk assessment** that considers attack scenarios, such as systems directly reachable from the internet (VPN, webmail, M365, remote desktop); and the method is consistent with recognized security best practices.","In other words, if your M365 is open to the internet and uses only passwords, it is hard to show that you considered internet attack scenarios and follow best practices. Note: commonly cited best practices include NIST SP 800-63B and BSI recommendations. Today's mainstream view focuses on password length and checking against leaked passwords, rather than forcing frequent changes. ISA 2027.1 deliberately changed \"state-of-the-art\" to \"proven and widely accepted\", so organizations can use such recognized standards as their basis.","The should requirements: define authentication procedures based on business and security requirements, use at least strong passwords, and use superior methods (PAM, 2FA) for privileged accounts. High protection needs (C, I, A) add supplementary measures depending on risk, such as monitoring for unusual logins, automatic logout, disabling after inactivity, account lockout or brute-force protection. Very high protection needs (C, I) require strong authentication, for example two-factor authentication, before accessing data with very high protection needs.","A common misunderstanding is \"we have MFA, so we are done\". Auditors will ask: which systems have MFA? What about OT systems, local server administrators, network devices and cloud consoles? How are service accounts handled? The choice of authentication method must be supported by a documented risk assessment and should be looked at together with 4.1.3 account management and 4.2.1 access rights management."],"practice":["Create a \"system authentication overview\": for each system (AD, M365, VPN, ERP, MES, PLM, firewall, cloud console), whether it is internet-facing, its protection needs, the authentication method used, whether MFA is used, and the risk assessment basis.","Enable MFA for all internet-facing services (M365, VPN, remote access). Prefer authenticator push with number matching or FIDO2, and avoid SMS-only methods.","Base the password policy on recognized practice, for example a minimum length of 12 characters or more, blocking common and leaked passwords (Entra Password Protection), and an account lockout threshold.","Privileged accounts: administrators use separate privileged accounts with mandatory MFA; critical systems use PAM (for example CyberArk, Delinea, or LAPS plus a jump server); privileged sessions are recorded or logged.","Supplementary measures for high protection needs: conditional access (only compliant devices, block logins from high-risk countries), alerts on unusual logins sent to the SIEM, automatic screen lock after 10–15 minutes of inactivity, and automatic logout from applications when idle.","For data with very high protection needs (for example customer project folders or confidential PLM projects), enforce MFA or a dedicated environment before access.","Once a year, check whether the authentication methods still meet best practices, and document this in the management review or risk assessment."],"audit":{"questions":["Which user authentication methods does the organization use?","What is the basis for choosing the authentication methods (risk assessment, best practices, business and security requirements)?","Which methods are used to authenticate privileged user accounts?","How do you make sure the password standard used by the organization matches current best practices?","Have sensitive resources been identified? Which sensitive resources and systems are accessed with two-factor authentication?","How do you make sure privileged access rights are restricted, controlled and protected by stronger authentication?","Has information with very high protection needs been identified, and is access to it protected with a second factor?"],"evidence":["Risk assessment of authentication methods or the system authentication overview","Password policy and screenshots of AD or Entra ID password settings","MFA enrollment status report and conditional access policies","PAM system configuration and list of privileged accounts","Screenshots of account lockout and idle logout settings","Records of alerts on unusual logins"],"findings":["VPN and webmail are open to the internet with username and password only, and no risk assessment was done.","Normal users have MFA, but domain administrator and firewall administrator accounts do not.","The password policy requires only 6 characters, which does not match recognized best practices.","Data with very high protection needs has not been identified, so nobody can explain which access requires a second factor.","There is no record of why the authentication methods were chosen; the only answer is \"that is the vendor default\"."]},"tip":"Allan's tip: The places where MFA is most often missing are \"administrators\" and \"network devices\", because everyone thinks IT's own people will not cause problems. But these are exactly the accounts hackers want most."},{"id":"4.1.3","title":"Secure management of user accounts and login information","q_en":"To what extent are user accounts and login information securely managed and applied?","q_zh":"To what extent are user accounts and login information securely managed and applied?","objective":"Access to information and IT systems is provided via validated user accounts assigned to a person. It is important to protect login information and to ensure the traceability of transactions and accesses.","req":{"must":["The creating, changing, and deleting of user accounts is conducted.","Unique and personalized user accounts are used.","The use of “shared accounts” is regulated (e.g., restricted to cases where traceability of actions is dispensable).","User accounts are disabled immediately after the user has resigned from or left the organization (e.g., upon termination of the employment contract).","User accounts are regularly reviewed.","The login information is provided to the user in a secure manner.","A policy for the handling of login information is defined and implemented. The following aspects are considered: – No disclosure of login information, not even to persons of authority, within the limits of applicable law; – No writing down or unencrypted storing of login information; – Immediate changing of login information whenever potential compromising is suspected; – No use of identical login information for business and non-business purposes; – Changing of temporary or initial login information following the 1st login; – Requirements for the quality of authentication information (e.g., length of password, types of characters to be used)."],"should":["A basic user account with minimum access rights and functionalities is existent and used.","Default accounts and passwords pre-configured by manufacturers are disabled (e.g., by blocking or changing of password).","User accounts are created or authorized by the responsible body.","Creating user accounts is subject to an approval process (four-eyes principle).","User accounts of service providers are disabled upon completion of their task.","Deadlines for disabling and deleting user accounts are defined.","The use of default passwords is technically prevented.","Where strong authentication is applied, the use of the medium (e.g., ownership factor) is secure.","User accounts are reviewed at regular intervals. This also includes user accounts in customers' IT systems.","Interactive login for service accounts (technical accounts) is technically prevented."],"high":[],"vhigh":[],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.5.16, A.5.17, A.5.18, A.8.9 | NIST CSF 2.0 PR.AA-04 | BSI IT-Grundschutz ORP.4, OPS.1.1.2 | NIST SP800-53r5 AC-2(2), IA-2, IA-4, IA-5(13), IA-6, IA-7, IA-8, IA-11, AC-16, AC-20, CA-9, SC-2","change2027":"","explain":["4.1.3 has the most must requirements in Chapter 4. The core idea is **one person, one account; every account has a lifecycle; passwords follow rules**. Accounts must be unique and personal so you can trace \"who did what\". Shared accounts may only be used where traceability is not needed, and must be regulated, for example a read-only account on a production dashboard PC.","Lifecycle: creating, changing and deleting accounts needs a process. Leaver accounts must be disabled \"immediately\". This is the item auditors check most often, usually by comparing the list of leavers with the deactivation dates in AD or M365. Accounts must be reviewed regularly. Initial passwords must be delivered securely, for example in person, through separate channels or with a one-time link — never written in an e-mail to the manager to pass on.","The login information policy must cover six points: no disclosure (not even to managers or IT), no writing down or unencrypted storage (for example sticky notes or plain-text Excel files), immediate change if a leak is suspected, no use of the same password for business and private purposes, change of the initial password at first login, and password quality requirements. These six points are usually written in a \"password management policy\" and made known to employees through the 2.1.3 training.","The ten should requirements are worth checking one by one. Items often overlooked are: manufacturer default accounts (for example admin/admin on network devices, printers, PLCs and IP cameras), disabling service provider accounts when their task is finished, reviewing accounts in customer IT systems (for example an employee's account on an OEM supplier portal — when the employee leaves, the customer must be asked to disable it), and technically preventing interactive login for service accounts."],"practice":["Create an \"account management procedure\": request, approval (requester's manager plus system owner, four-eyes principle), creation, change, deactivation and deletion. Define deadlines for deactivation (for example disabled on the last day, deleted after 90 days).","Integrate the HR system with AD or Entra ID, or let the leaver form trigger same-day deactivation by IT. Every month, compare the HR leaver list with account status.","Shared account register: purpose, users, why traceability is not needed, and compensating measures (for example read-only, limited to specific terminals), approved by the security officer.","Generate initial passwords randomly, deliver them through separate channels (for example the account name by e-mail and the password by text message), and force a change at first login.","Create a \"password and login information policy\" that covers the six must points, and roll out a company-approved password manager (for example Bitwarden, 1Password or Keeper).","Add \"change or disable default accounts and passwords\" to the checklist for commissioning new equipment, including OT devices, network devices, printers and IP cameras.","For service accounts, use GPO settings \"Deny log on locally\" and \"Deny log on through Remote Desktop Services\", or use gMSA.","Review accounts every six months (including customer portals and supplier remote maintenance accounts); the system owner signs off the review records."],"audit":{"questions":["How are user accounts created, changed, blocked and deleted? Are unique, personal accounts used?","What security measures are in place where shared accounts are used?","Are user accounts reviewed regularly? Can you show the most recent review record?","How is login information delivered to users?","Which rules for handling login information have been defined and implemented?","What training have employees received on handling login information?","What security considerations ensure that login information is not disclosed?","Please compare last month's leaver list with the AD account deactivation dates."],"evidence":["Account management procedure and account request forms (with approval records)","AD or Entra ID account list and last-logon report","Comparison of the leaver list with account deactivation dates","Shared account register and approval records","Password and login information policy","Account review records (including accounts in customer systems)","GPO settings that prevent interactive login for service accounts"],"findings":["Accounts of former employees were still active several weeks after they left, and some even show logins.","Production and quality staff log in to the MES with shared accounts that can change data; the operator cannot be traced, and this is not regulated.","IT sends initial passwords in plain text by e-mail and does not force a change at first login.","Network switches and PLCs still use factory default passwords.","Employee accounts on customer portals have never been reviewed, and former employees can still log in to customer systems."]},"tip":"Allan's tip: When I worked as an auditor, leaver account deactivation was the check with the highest hit rate. Ask IT to compare the list with HR once a month. It takes ten minutes and can save you a finding."},{"id":"4.2.1","title":"Assignment and management of access rights","q_en":"To what extent are access rights assigned and managed?","q_zh":"To what extent are access rights assigned and managed?","objective":"The management of access rights ensures that only authorized users have access to information and IT services. For this purpose, access rights are assigned to user accounts.","req":{"must":["The requirements for the management of access rights (authorization) are determined and fulfilled. The following aspects are considered: – Procedure for application, verification, and approval; – Applying the minimum (“need-to-know”/\"least privilege\") principle; – Access rights are revoked when no longer needed.","The access rights granted for normal and privileged user accounts and technical accounts are reviewed at regular intervals also within IT systems of customers."],"should":["Strategies for authorizing access to information are prepared.","Authorization roles are used.","Rights are allocated on a need-to-use basis and according to the role and/or area of responsibility.","Normal user accounts are not granted privileged access rights.","The user’s access rights are updated after the user’s responsibilities have changed (e.g., to another field of responsibility)."],"high":["The access rights are approved by the responsible internal Information Officer. (C, I, A)"],"vhigh":["Information is stored in encrypted form at content level (e.g., file level) to prevent unauthorized persons from gaining access and information (privileged users). Where encryption is not feasible, information shall be protected by similarly effective measures. (C)","Existing access rights are regularly reviewed at shorter intervals (e.g., quarterly). (C)"],"sga":[]},"refs":"ISO 27001:2022 A.5.18 | ISA/IEC 62443-2-1 11.2.4, 11.3.1, 11.3.4 | NIST CSF 2.0 PR.AA-05 | BSI IT-Grundschutz ORP.4, OPS.1.1.2 | NIST SP800-53r5 AC-2(3), AC-2(7), AC-3, AC-4, AC-6, AC-19, CA-3(6), CA-6, IA-11, MP-2, PS-4, PS-5","change2027":"","explain":["4.1.3 manages the \"account\"; 4.2.1 manages \"what the account can do\". The must requirement asks for a process for requesting, verifying and approving access rights, following the minimum principle (need-to-know and least privilege), and revoking rights when they are no longer needed. The rights of normal, privileged and technical accounts must be reviewed regularly — including the rights in customer IT systems.","The should requirements recommend managing rights with **roles** (RBAC), for example a \"sales assistant\" role that can view orders in the ERP but cannot change prices; allocating rights on a need-to-use basis and by responsibility; not giving privileged rights to normal accounts (for example the everyday account should not be a local administrator); and updating rights when people change jobs, to avoid \"privilege creep\" — the longer someone stays and the more departments they move through, the more rights they collect.","High protection needs (C, I, A) require approval of access rights by the responsible internal information officer, meaning the owner of the information (for example the project manager or the head of design) agrees — not IT deciding on its own. Very high protection needs (C) add two items: information is encrypted at content level so that even IT administrators cannot see the content, for example with Microsoft Purview sensitivity label encryption or encrypted containers; and access rights are reviewed at shorter intervals (for example quarterly).","A common mistake is to turn the \"access review\" into IT printing a list and stamping it. An effective review is done by the information owner or the department manager, who confirms \"do these people still need these rights?\" Rights that are no longer needed are actually removed, and this is recorded. This control supports and is supported by 1.3.2 information classification, 4.1.3 account management and 5.2.4 log monitoring."],"practice":["Create an \"access rights management procedure\" that defines the fields of the request form (requester, system, role, reason, duration, manager approval, information owner approval, IT execution).","Build a \"role and rights matrix\": define standard roles by department and job for the file server, ERP, PLM, MES and M365 SharePoint. New hires get rights based on their role.","Grant rights on file servers and SharePoint through AD groups, not to individuals directly. The project manager is the owner of each project folder.","Administrators use separate privileged accounts, and local administrator rights are removed from everyday accounts (for example with LAPS or Intune Endpoint Privilege Management).","Transfer process: HR sends a transfer notice, and IT resets rights for the new role and removes the old rights.","Access reviews: normal systems every six or twelve months, privileged accounts every quarter. The information owner confirms and signs off, and removals are recorded. Include employee rights in customer systems (such as OEM portals and customer VPNs).","Very high protection needs: use Purview sensitivity labels or file encryption tools for content-level encryption, restrict privileged administrators from accessing content, and review rights every quarter."],"audit":{"questions":["What is the process for requesting, verifying and approving access rights? Please show three sampled request records.","How do you implement the least privilege principle? Is there a role and rights matrix?","When an employee changes jobs, how are the rights from the old department removed?","When was the last access review? Who did it? How were the issues found handled?","Are the rights of privileged and service accounts included in the review? What about rights in customer systems?","(High protection needs) Are access rights approved by the information owner?","(Very high protection needs) How do you prevent IT administrators from seeing the content of strictly confidential data?"],"evidence":["Access rights management procedure and request forms","Role and rights matrix","AD group membership reports, file server permission reports","Access review records and records of removals","Records of rights changes after transfers","Content-level encryption configuration (such as sensitivity label policies)","Review records for account rights in customer systems"],"findings":["Access rights are requested only verbally or by chat message (for example WhatsApp, LINE or Teams); there are no formal request and approval records.","Many folders on the file server are shared with Everyone or Domain Users, which violates the least privilege principle.","Employees keep the rights of their old department after a transfer, leading to privilege creep.","Access reviews are done by IT alone, without confirmation by the information owners, and do not cover service accounts or customer systems.","Everyday user accounts have domain administrator rights."]},"tip":"Allan's tip: \"Everyone\" in folder permissions is an auditor's favorite. Before the assessment, scan the file server permissions with a tool. You will get a shock — but better now than during the assessment."}],"summary":["4.1.1: Identification means (keys, access cards, tokens) need full lifecycle management: creation, handover, return and destruction, validity periods, traceability and handling of loss. For high protection needs, have a strategy to block or invalidate them when lost.","4.1.2: Choose authentication methods based on a risk assessment, consider internet attack scenarios, and follow recognized best practices. Use PAM or 2FA for privileged accounts. High: add monitoring, automatic logout and brute-force protection. Very high: strong authentication before access.","4.1.3: One person, one account; rules for shared accounts; immediate deactivation when people leave; regular reviews; secure delivery of initial passwords; a login information policy with six points. Should covers default passwords, the four-eyes principle, no interactive login for service accounts, and reviews of accounts in customer systems.","4.2.1: Access rights need a request, review and approval process, least privilege, revocation when no longer needed, and regular reviews (including privileged and technical accounts and customer systems). High: approval by the information owner. Very high: content-level encryption and quarterly reviews.","Most common findings: leaver accounts not disabled, no MFA for administrators, shared accounts without traceability, folders shared with Everyone, and access reviews done only on paper."]}]});
